Cyde Weys wrote:

> The feats will be listing in this format
> Name: The name of the feat
> Reqs: The requirements to accomplish it
> Rews: Your reward for completing this feat

> Name: Nymphslayer
> Reqs: Kill a total of 20 nymphs (any species).
> Rews: When a nymph steals money from you, there is a chance that you
> grab onto it and prevent him from teleporting away with your money.

Nymphs don't steal money. And they're female.

> Name: Master Jeweler
> Reqs: Identify all gems.
> Rews: {Anyone got any ideas?}

You notice buried gems when you walk over them (or dig a corridor over
them).

> Name: Fire Eater
> Reqs: Eat anything that is on fire, e.g. lit candle, lamp, etc.

This is getting ridiculous.

> Name: Fortune Teller
> Reqs: Eat 5 fortune cookies.
> Rews: Get minor oracularities instead of random messages when eating
> fortune cookies.

You mean only getting true fortunes? That's what minor oracularities
are.

> Name: Miner
> Reqs: Dig 250 spaces.
> Rews: Reduces the amount of time required to dig.

What's the achievement in digging 250 spaces? I do that in almost every
game.

> Name: Skilled Miner
> Reqs: Dig 750 spaces.
> Rews: Halves the amount of time required to dig.
> Name: Master Miner
> Reqs: Dig 1500 spaces.
> Rews: Reduces the amount of time required to dig to 1 turn.


Way too strong. Digging should take lots of time.

> Name: Altruist
> Reqs: Heal 10 hostile monsters.
> Rews: Gives a bonus to healing yourself.

Again, way too strong.

> Name: Glutton
> Reqs: Eat 50 food rations.
> Rews: The amount of nutrition given from eating a food ration is
> increased.

This should give a penalty: you're getting resistant to food.  :-)

> Name: Worm Rider
> Reqs: Kill 10 long worms.
> Rews: Gives a bonus to taming long worms and allows you to ride them
> (is that possible?).

It should be possible, on the first part (otherwise, coding it would be
nigh impossible), but I don't think it is.

> Name: Equineist
> Reqs: Feed 20 items of food to horses.
> Rews: Increases chance of successfully taming a horse, #chatting with
> a horse gives you a small chance of taming it.

Why not for other pets? And the name should be horsewhisperer, of
course.

> Name: Marksman
> Reqs: Kill 20 monsters with thrown or fired weapons, e.g. arrows,
> daggers, etc.
> Rews: A to-hit bonus on thrown weapons.

Classes that are good with these weapons already get bonuses with them,
since they can become expert in them.

> Name: Innate Defense
> Reqs: Reach -9 naked AC (usually by donating to priests).
> Rews: Get an additional -1 AC.
> *Note, this one would be REALLY hard to get!

??? I often reach this at level 1, with the protection racket. And
anyone with only a little bit of patience will always reach this. It
would just mean that the last point of easy gain is doubled.

> Name: Agnostic
> Reqs: Change alignment twice.
> Rews: {Anyone got any ideas?}

This shouldn't be rewarded, but penalised.

> Name: Transgender Warrior
> Reqs: Change genders.
> Rews: {Anyone got any ideas?}

Why should an accident be rewarded?

> As you can see, the point of the feats is to reward you for going out
> of your way and doing things you wouldn't normally do.

I beg to differ here. Most of these things happen in the majority of my
games, so they would just give me free bonuses, making the game much
easier for advanced characters. There are no disadvantages at all, so
game balance would be lost, I think.

Of course, there are some things that you wouldn't indeed do normally,
like eating a cockatrice, but to set up a system like this for only the
few such things you mention would be over the top, I guess.

> As it currently is, the only reason you'd not want to genocide certain
> monsters is if you're going with extinctionless conduct.  That's not a
> very good reason.  If, however, there were useful feats that could be
> gained by not taking the chicken route of genociding the tough
> species, then there might be less incentive to genocide everything
> willy-nilly.

You'd just genocide them a bit later, of forfeit the small advantage
that not genociding them gains you.

And what's the use of not genociding cockatrices so your chance of being
stoned gets a tiny bit smaller, when genociding them reduces the chance
to zero?

> So, what do you think?  I might be interested in actually coding this
> one up myself, if no one else beats me to it.  It would take a lot of
> work though.

Actually, I don't like it. It would greatly disrupt game balance, making
the game *much* easier for higher level characters. This could be
countered by making high level monsters tougher, but then, the game
would become even more difficult for players who are new to the late
game.

So the game would become easier for experienced players, and more
difficult for beginning players, while it should actually be the other
way around.

